[{"id":"30771","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What was meant by the term \"commanding heights of the economy\" in the second five year plan of India?</span></p>","option_1":"A) Complete control over the vital industries by government which are important for economy.","option_2":"B) Complete control on the economy by the private players.","option_3":"C) Promotion of free trade agreement.","option_4":"D) Promotion of foreign direct investment in vital industries.","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - A) Complete control over the vital industries by government which are important for economy..</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Second Five Year Plan (1956-61) was based on the Mahalanobis model, which emphasised rapid industrialisation with a focus on heavy and basic industries.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The phrase \"commanding heights of the economy\" referred to the strategy of giving the state (public sector) control over key/basic and heavy industries (like steel, power, and machine-building), so that the government could direct the overall pace and pattern of economic development, while the private sector operated in the remaining areas.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30772","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Muttaraiyars of Cauvery delta was subordinate to whom?</span></p>","option_1":"A) Chalukyas of Kalyani","option_2":"B) Pallava kings of Kanchipuram","option_3":"C) Pandyas of Madurai","option_4":"D) Chola kings of Uraiyar","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - B) Pallava kings of Kanchipuram.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Muttaraiyars were a local Tamil ruling family who controlled the Cauvery delta region (around present-day Pudukkottai) from about the 7th to the 9th century CE.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>They functioned as feudatories/subordinates of the Pallava kings of Kanchipuram, and later lost their territory to the resurgent imperial Cholas under Vijayalaya in the 9th century CE.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30773","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In 1565, the Mughal forces under Asaf Khan attacked _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Malwa","option_2":"Garha Katanga","option_3":"Deccan","option_4":"Chittorgarh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Garha Katanga.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Asaf Khan, the Mughal governor of Kara (under Akbar), invaded the kingdom of Garha-Katanga (in present-day Madhya Pradesh), then ruled by the regent Rani Durgavati on behalf of her minor son.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Durgavati was defeated and died fighting in 1564, and the kingdom was formally annexed by the Mughals by 1565.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30774","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which Veda contains magical spells and charms?</span></p>","option_1":"Rigveda","option_2":"Samaveda","option_3":"Yajurveda","option_4":"Atharvaveda","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Atharvaveda.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Atharvaveda, traditionally attributed to sage Atharvan, is distinct from the other three Vedas as it deals with everyday practical life — including magical spells, charms, incantations, and remedies for diseases, enemies, and misfortune, alongside philosophical hymns.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Rigveda contains hymns of praise to deities, the Samaveda contains musical chants derived largely from the Rigveda, and the Yajurveda contains sacrificial formulae used in rituals.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30775","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>With respect to the stomata, what should be written in place of \"A\"? (Refer to the diagram given in the question.)</span></p>","option_1":"Stomatal pore","option_2":"Mitochondria","option_3":"Nucleus","option_4":"Lamina","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Stomatal pore.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A stoma (plural stomata) is a minute pore present in the epidermis of leaves, flanked by two bean-shaped guard cells.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The gap/opening enclosed between the two guard cells — labelled \"A\" in the diagram — is called the stomatal pore (or stomatal aperture), through which gaseous exchange (CO2, O2) and transpiration (loss of water vapour) take place.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30776","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The city administration of Patliputra, according to Megasthenes, was conducted by six committees of _____ members each.</span></p>","option_1":"3","option_2":"4","option_3":"5","option_4":"6","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 5.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Megasthenes, the Greek ambassador at the Mauryan court, in his account Indica, described the municipal administration of Pataliputra as being carried out by six boards (committees), each consisting of five members — a total of thirty officials.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Each board looked after a specific area of civic administration, such as industrial arts, foreigners, registration of births and deaths, trade and commerce, manufactured goods, and collection of taxes on sold goods.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30777","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which Indian state is known for the folk dance 'Chohara'?</span></p>","option_1":"Maharashtra","option_2":"Andhra Pradesh","option_3":"Karnataka","option_4":"Himachal Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Himachal Pradesh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Chohara is a traditional folk dance of Himachal Pradesh, performed by both men and women, often during festivals and social occasions in the hill state.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Himachal Pradesh is also known for other folk dances such as Nati, Dangi, and Thoda, which are frequently asked about in SSC General Awareness sections.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30778","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Thiruvathirakali' is a popular dance of which of the following Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Odisha","option_2":"Kerala","option_3":"Maharashtra","option_4":"Telangana","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Kerala.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Thiruvathirakali (also spelt Thiruvathira Kali) is a group dance performed by women in Kerala, traditionally associated with the Thiruvathira festival celebrated in the Malayalam month of Dhanu, in devotion to Lord Shiva.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Dancers move in a circle around a lamp (nilavilakku), clapping in rhythm while singing devotional songs — it is closely linked with Onam and Thiruvathira celebrations in Kerala.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30779","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Indra Jatra' festival is celebrated in which of the following Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Sikkim","option_2":"Goa","option_3":"Gujarat","option_4":"Maharashtra","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Sikkim.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Indra Jatra is a festival honouring Indra, the god of rain, and is widely celebrated by Newar communities, and in parts of Sikkim in India with a sizeable Nepali-origin population.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The festival involves worship of Indra along with cultural processions and traditional dances, and is an important part of the region's socio-religious calendar.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30780","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which state of India is the festival of 'Chikalkalo' celebrated?</span></p>","option_1":"Sikkim","option_2":"Haryana","option_3":"Goa","option_4":"Odisha","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Goa.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Chikal Kalo (meaning 'mud play') is a unique festival celebrated in Marcel village in Goa, usually around Krishna Janmashtami.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Villagers, especially farmers, smear each other with mud from paddy fields as part of the celebration, which is linked to agricultural traditions and the sowing season, and is followed by the Gulal Utsav (colour festival) the next day.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30781","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Marginal Worker in India is a person who works for less than_____ days in a year.</span></p>","option_1":"83","option_2":"90","option_3":"100","option_4":"183","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - 183.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per the Census of India classification, workers are divided into Main Workers and Marginal Workers based on the duration of work in the reference year.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A Marginal Worker is one who has worked for less than 183 days (i.e., less than 6 months) in the year preceding the Census; those who worked for 183 days or more are classified as Main Workers.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30782","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Under the Regulating Act of 1773, court of appeal \"the Sadar Nizamat Adalat\" was set up at which of the following city in India?</span></p>","option_1":"Bombay","option_2":"Calcutta","option_3":"Mysore","option_4":"Lucknow","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Calcutta.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Regulating Act of 1773 reorganised the administration of the East India Company's territories and established a system of courts.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Sadar Nizamat Adalat (the highest court of criminal appeal) and the Sadar Diwani Adalat (the highest court of civil appeal) were both established at Calcutta, which was also made the seat of the Governor-General of Bengal under this Act.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30783","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who among the following founded the reform association called Arya Samaj?</span></p>","option_1":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar","option_2":"Raja Rammohun Roy","option_3":"Swami Dayanand Saraswati","option_4":"Radhakanta Deb","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Swami Dayanand Saraswati.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Arya Samaj was founded by Swami Dayanand Saraswati in Bombay in 1875, with its motto 'Krinvanto Vishwaram Aryam' (Make this world noble).</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The organisation aimed to reform Hindu society by rejecting idol worship, caste discrimination and superstitions, and by advocating a return to the authority of the Vedas. Raja Rammohun Roy, in contrast, founded the Brahmo Samaj in 1828.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30784","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> @font-face { font-family: 'Shruti';src: url('https://appxcontent.kaxa.in/fonts/shruti.ttf') format('truetype');} </style><style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which Gharana is the classical singer Shruti Sadolikar associated with?</span></p>","option_1":"Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana","option_2":"Bhendibazaar Gharana","option_3":"Agra Gharana","option_4":"Patiala Gharana","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> @font-face { font-family: 'Shruti';src: url('https://appxcontent.kaxa.in/fonts/shruti.ttf') format('truetype');} </style><style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Shruti Sadolikar Katkar is a renowned Hindustani classical vocalist belonging to the Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana, a gharana known for its emphasis on complex raga elaboration and rhythmic patterns, originally founded by Ustad Alladiya Khan.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>She is a recipient of the Padma Bhushan and has been a leading exponent of this gharana in contemporary Indian classical music.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30785","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is reverberation?</span></p>","option_1":"The initial sound heard before reflection","option_2":"The persistence of sound due to repeated reflections from walls","option_3":"The absorption of sound by materials","option_4":"The amplification of sound in a small space","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - The persistence of sound due to repeated reflections from walls.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Reverberation is the phenomenon of persistence or prolongation of sound in a space (like a hall or room) even after the original source of sound has stopped, caused by multiple reflections of sound waves off walls, ceiling, and floor.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Excessive reverberation makes speech and music indistinct, so auditoriums are designed with sound-absorbing materials to control the reverberation time to an optimum level.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30786","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is NOT an example of Plantation crop?</span></p>","option_1":"Tea","option_2":"Rubber","option_3":"Coffee","option_4":"Bajra","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Bajra.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Plantation crops are cash crops grown on large estates, usually as monoculture, and typically include tea, coffee, rubber, coconut, and cocoa, which require heavy capital investment and are largely meant for commercial/export purposes.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Bajra (pearl millet) is a coarse cereal (kharif food crop) grown mainly in dry, semi-arid regions of India, and is not classified as a plantation crop.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30787","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In the context of Balance of Payment, which of the following statement is correct regarding Balance of Trade (BOT)?</span></p>","option_1":"It is the difference between the value of exports and value of imports of a country in a given period of time.","option_2":"Export of goods is entered as a debit item in BOT.","option_3":"Imports of goods is entered as a credit item in BOT.","option_4":"BOT is said to be in surplus when exports of goods are equal to the imports of goods.","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - It is the difference between the value of exports and value of imports of a country in a given period of time..</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Balance of Trade (BOT), also called the trade balance, is defined as the difference between the monetary value of a country's exports and imports of goods (visible/merchandise trade) over a given period of time.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In BOT accounting, exports are recorded as credit items (inflow) and imports as debit items (outflow); a country has a trade surplus when exports exceed imports, and a trade deficit when imports exceed exports — not when they are equal.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30788","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'The Rediscovery of India' has been authored by _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Meghnad Desai","option_2":"Pavan K. Varma","option_3":"Tabish Khair","option_4":"Meera Syal","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Meghnad Desai.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'The Rediscovery of India' is a book written by economist and author Lord Meghnad Desai, offering a re-examination of India's history and identity.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It should not be confused with Jawaharlal Nehru's famous work 'The Discovery of India', which is a distinct and separate book, a distinction that is frequently tested in SSC exams.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30789","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who won the AutHer Awards 2023 in the Best Fiction category for the novel 'Everything the Light Touches'?</span></p>","option_1":"Avni Doshi","option_2":"Janice Pariat","option_3":"Arundhati Roy","option_4":"Anuja Chauhan","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Janice Pariat.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The AutHer Awards, instituted by JCB Prize backers/Women's Web, recognise outstanding literary works by Indian women authors across categories.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Janice Pariat won the Best Fiction category at the AutHer Awards 2023 for her novel 'Everything the Light Touches', which weaves together the stories of four characters connected by their relationship with plants and nature.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30790","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following UNESCO World Heritage Sites is located in Gujarat?</span></p>","option_1":"Khajuraho Group of Monuments","option_2":"Group of Monuments at Hampi","option_3":"Champaner-Pavagadh Archaeological Park","option_4":"Ajanta caves","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Champaner-Pavagadh Archaeological Park.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Champaner-Pavagadh Archaeological Park, located in Panchmahal district of Gujarat, was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2004.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It is a unique complex of Chalcolithic sites, hill fortresses, and the 16th-century capital of Sultan Mahmud Begda. In contrast, Khajuraho is in Madhya Pradesh, Hampi is in Karnataka, and Ajanta Caves are in Maharashtra.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30791","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who built the tomb of Itmad-ud-Daula in Agra?</span></p>","option_1":"Nur Jahan","option_2":"Mumtaz Mahal","option_3":"Akbar","option_4":"Shah Jahan","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Nur Jahan.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Tomb of Itmad-ud-Daula was built by Empress Nur Jahan, wife of Emperor Jahangir, in memory of her father, Mirza Ghiyas Beg (who held the title Itmad-ud-Daula).</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Built between 1622 and 1628 on the banks of the Yamuna in Agra, it is often called the 'Baby Taj' and is notable as one of the earliest Mughal structures built entirely of white marble with intricate pietra dura inlay work, a style later refined in the Taj Mahal.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30792","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>How does the increased level of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere contribute to global warming?</span></p>","option_1":"By increasing cloud cover","option_2":"By trapping heat rays reflected by the earth","option_3":"By causing more snowfall","option_4":"By cooling the earth's surface","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - By trapping heat rays reflected by the earth.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Carbon dioxide is a greenhouse gas — it allows incoming shortwave solar radiation to pass through the atmosphere but absorbs and re-radiates the outgoing longwave (infrared/heat) radiation reflected by the Earth's surface.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This trapping of heat within the atmosphere, known as the greenhouse effect, raises the average global temperature; rising anthropogenic CO2 emissions from burning fossil fuels have intensified this effect, leading to global warming.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30793","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following group of musical instruments are known as Aerophones?</span></p>","option_1":"Ghan Vadya","option_2":"Tat Vadya","option_3":"Avanaddha Vadya","option_4":"Sushir Vadya","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Sushir Vadya.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In the traditional Indian classification of musical instruments, Sushir Vadya refers to wind instruments (aerophones) in which sound is produced by vibrating a column of air, such as the flute (bansuri) and shehnai.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The other categories are: Tat Vadya (chordophones/string instruments, e.g. sitar, veena), Avanaddha Vadya (membranophones, e.g. tabla, mridangam), and Ghan Vadya (idiophones/solid, self-sounding instruments, e.g. manjira, jal-tarang).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30794","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum looks rough under a microscope because it has particles called _____ attached to its surface.</span></p>","option_1":"plastid","option_2":"chromosomes","option_3":"ribosomes","option_4":"cytoplasm","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - ribosomes.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum (RER) gets its granular, 'rough' appearance under an electron microscope because numerous ribosomes are studded on its outer (cytoplasmic) surface.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>These ribosomes are the sites of protein synthesis; proteins synthesised here are typically meant for secretion or for use in membranes and organelles. The Smooth ER, by contrast, lacks ribosomes and appears smooth, being involved chiefly in lipid synthesis and detoxification.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30795","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"820","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is the use of kerosene, a constituent of petroleum?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Fuel for stoves</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Fuel for lamps</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Kerosene (or kerosene oil) is a middle fraction obtained during the fractional distillation of crude petroleum, distilling between the diesel and petrol/naphtha fractions.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It is widely used as a domestic fuel for cooking stoves and as fuel for lighting lamps, especially in rural and semi-urban households, making both statements I and II correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]